<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The test project sees internals, and that is a deliberate trade rather than a
    convenience. The alternative to narrowing these types was leaving the search
    engine, the reservation ring and the heap in the library's public API, where
    every one of them is an implementation detail a consumer should never name.

    It is worth being honest about what this costs. InternalsVisibleTo is
    assembly-wide, so it widens access for production code inside Nav.Core too,
    not only for tests. C# offers no narrower grant: there is no per-type
    friendship, and nothing like the in-module test block other languages provide.

    Where a seam is worth designing rather than merely reaching through, the
    answer is an interface a test can implement instead. That is why IGroupOps,
    IReservationView, IDistanceFieldSource and IChokepointSource exist, and why
    the doctrine tests need no privileged access at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Nav.Core.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
